twilight princess HD is a ten-year-old  game that's been given a facelift which  is to say it wraps sharp new textures  around its polygons everything else is  old the audio synthesized doing things  like climbing ladders can be a bit of a  fuss and the gameplay overall lacks the  convenience of modern action RPGs but  overall there's actually something  quaint and wonderful about the old  fashioned way that Twilight Princess  plays in the ten years since it was  first released on the Gamecube and we  most adventure games have become sort of  obsessed with being a slick and  streamlined as possible games have  become absolutely terrified that you'll  get lost so you have all these gimmicks  to keep you on track things like the  mission checklist on your pip-boy in  fallout 4 or the sat-nav system that  guides you through the witcher 3  Twilight Princess doesn't have any of  that it's old-school insofar as it  leaves you to your own devices hands  unhealth  sometimes it's straight up doesn't tell  you where to go next  it forces you to explore to investigate  ask around play curious what a red joy  it is to be lost in hyrule searching  across its fields and mountains and  towns and rivers in pursuit of something  how old this approaches but how new it  feels there's a serendipitous bliss to  the details you make throughout your  main quest you'll stumble into dark  alleyways secret catacombs niche  boutiques a village straight out of a  spaghetti western and a magnificent  stone bridge about a quarter mile across  not since Ocarina of Time has a Zelda  game offered a world of this scale and  activity with so much confidence and  it's the residents of this world who  make every journey seem worthwhile  Twilight Princess sees nearly every  character as an opportunity to inject  more personality and charisma into its  world the gorons are aggressive and  territorial locals of Death Mountain the  Canon operator is a downtrodden clown  with bulbus pitiful eyes the Yeti up in  the wintry north has an adorably  gigantic bum and speaks in all cow  the hyrulean town beggar promises good  fortune if you keep giving him rupees  and just as a word of warning I've spent  about 700 bucks on the guy so far and I  haven't got anything back then there's  Zant the Magnificent mad sidekick of  Ganondorf who plays as chief antagonist  for the majority of the story I just  couldn't take my eyes off him Sant is  unbelievably sinister and weird in an  enigmatic sort of way and that can be  more appropriate than Twilight Princess  this game is dark it's brutal as creepy  it features an alternate version of  Hyrule that is engulfed in shadow it's  wonderfully bleak the lighting is the  Mucky yellow of a cigarette stain while  squares of ash whizzed through the air  as though Nintendo had made a snow globe  from an ashtray  as link enters this Twilight Realm for  the first time he transforms into wolf  link later he can freely switch between  beast and human and this is clearly  Twilight Princess his big spin on the  old Zelda formula it's no disaster but  it doesn't quite work as well as it  needed to wolf Link's main attack is a  straightforward lunge or a tentatively a  charge spin which gives a battles of  pedestrian pace and a narrow scope of  strategy akame this is not fortunately  you can play as human link for most of  the 40-hour adventure and each of the  game's 9 dungeons will test links  arsenal of moves and weapons to their  very limits the dungeons it has to be  said are simply outstanding what amazes  me so much about them is how even the  most elaborate challenge is the kind  that tests your memory and lateral  thinking are built around a simple  question how do I spin that fan  how do I smashed that ice how do I reach  that platform in search of the answer  you will flood reams of water walk  upside down across cave ceilings lined  with quartz fashion a giant swing from a  sextet of placated baboons and carry a  cannibal across the icy halls of a  mountain peak mansion this is  quintessential Nintendo intricate  answers beautifully folded into a  straightforward question  unfortunately the dungeons and the game  as a whole is just far too easy don't be  too amazed if you never die once  throughout your entire quest there's a  new hero mode which makes things much  trickier but not really in a satisfying  way it's not as though the AI has  improved you just take more damage  Twyla princess HD is unmistakeably a  product of its time when it's released  in 2006 it wasn't just one thing it was  it was many things it was a love letter  to hardcore Zelda fans made during  anxious times for Nintendo it was  developed for to console simultaneously  it was the Gamecube last hurrah and it  was a weed launch title it was a  defining test for motion controls which  by the way have been stripped out so  because it was all these things and more  Twilight Princess has always had a sort  of warped identity perhaps now ten years  later it can finally be remembered how  it ought to be as a dark and brilliant  showpiece of a treasured series
 

